From f29c8abc6baa9319568151798a22d8bb1e6d3548 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 22:37:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] Bound a reopen only while the files are as the failure
 left them; never initialize over unreadable frames

Review of the header frame and the bounded reopen:

B1: another connection can recover a failed commit whole and checkpoint
it into the data file without changing the log's length or salt; the
bounded reopen then mixed the data file's copy of the failed commit with
a log bounded before it (half a transaction the caller saw fail). The
failure now records, besides the acknowledged end, the raw log from that
end on (hash), its salt, the data file's length and header, and its copy
of every page the failed transaction wrote (safepoints included); a
reopen is bounded only while all of these still match, otherwise the
files win, whole. The record is passed to RecordWriteFailure explicitly
instead of through Exception.Data (no stale bound from a reused
exception, no internal keys in the caller's exception), and the log is
read through a pooled reader by its exact length (an encrypted reader
that hit its end reads nothing more).

B2: an empty or missing data file beside a log whose header frame cannot
be read (frame 0 never written back while later frames were, or an
encrypted log opened without its password) was still initialized over,
and the WAL discarded. Such an open now refuses, changing neither file,
when the log holds any valid frame or an encryption preamble; a 5.0.21
legacy WAL beside an empty data file still recovers as before.

Also: an empty caller data stream is restored, not refused as missing;
note 9 said an encrypted header never written back is not restored, but
it reads as zeros and is (tested); the proven-log cache versions its
queue entries, so a path forgotten and proven again is not evicted first.

